 "narrative": "I assessed all newly delivered results under the same operational 10 percent rule used earlier: a clinically meaningful absolute mean difference is approximately 0.10 times the absolute overall outcome mean. For the outcomes examined here this is about 0.0256 for outcome_009, 0.0250 for outcome_015, 0.0250 for outcome_005, 0.0175 for outcome_006, and 0.0093 for outcome_014. All estimates and intervals were already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, and I did not multiply them again. The central conclusion remains unchanged: the only decisive, independently validated association is the very large feature_021 versus feature_021=0 effect on outcome_006 (H146); accepted stratum claims are coherent expressions of that same dataset-specific effect, not independent predictors.\n\nThe new conditional analyses did not produce a new clinically established association. H192 (outcome_009 feature_048 level_003 versus level_001, interaction by feature_019 level_002) has signed estimate +0.0283, with CI -0.0113 to +0.0680. The point estimate is near the outcome_009 threshold, but the interval includes both null and sub-threshold effects, and the interaction cells are smaller. This is not sufficient to claim effect modification, so I close it unresolved. H193 and H194 test whether the feature_065 contrast on outcome_015 is modified by, or present within, feature_021=1. H193 signed estimate is -0.0294, which is opposite the claimed negative interaction, and H194 in feature_021=1 is essentially null (signed -0.0023, wide interval). Together with R154 in feature_021=0, this pattern suggests the feature_065 signal, if real, is concentrated outside feature_021=1. I reject the negative interaction claim and the feature_021=1 association claim, while keeping H200 as a separate unresolved localization result. H195 (outcome_014 feature_065 interaction by feature_021=1) is positive in the claimed direction (+0.0579) but very imprecise and includes null; small conditional cells and multiplicity make acceptance inappropriate. H196 (outcome_005 feature_021 interaction by feature_019 level_002) has signed estimate +0.0165, below threshold and with CI crossing null, so I reject evidence of clinically meaningful modification. H197 tests whether feature_065 modifies the dominant feature_021 effect on outcome_006; the interaction is near zero (-0.0037), although the interval is wide. This provides no support for meaningful modification, though it does not prove absence because the interval includes large effects. I reject the modification claim as unsupported.\n\nThe most clinically relevant secondary signals are H198 and H200. H198 (outcome_009 feature_048 level_003 versus level_001 within feature_021=0) has signed estimate +0.0257, lower bound +0.0037. This excludes null but the lower bound is far below the threshold, so the data support a non-zero association but not a clinically established 10 percent effect. I keep it unresolved and close active follow-up on it. H200 (outcome_015 feature_065 within feature_019 level_002 and feature_021=0) has signed estimate +0.0326 with lower bound +0.0048. The point estimate exceeds threshold, but uncertainty includes substantial sub-threshold effects, so I do not accept it; it remains unresolved and is localized enough to merit one targeted interaction proposal. H199, within feature_021=0 on outcome_015, is below threshold and imprecise; I reject it. H201 is a very small, non-significant outcome_014 double-subgroup estimate; I reject it. H202 is positive but extremely wide (CI -0.0461 to +0.0755), and conditioning on higher log-PFS adds selection/collider concerns; I close it unresolved rather than accept. H204 is a broad screen with a near-zero signed estimate; I reject it.\n\nMethodologically, the dominant feature_021/outcome_006 effect is so large that multiplicity is unlikely to explain it, but it could still be a technical or population-structure axis rather than a causal or generalizable predictor. The new secondary candidates are much more vulnerable to confounding by correlated predictors, small conditional cells, post-selection bias from many subgroup tests, and ambiguous clinical relevance when outcome means are near zero (especially outcome_014). I did not observe any obvious coding or analysis leakage: cells meet the Welch cell-size rule, estimates are signed as instructed, and predictors are treated as measured baseline variables. Conditioning on feature_024 is treated cautiously because clinical PFS could be correlated with downstream dependency biology and can induce selection effects in observational associations.\n\nI requested no independent validation this iteration. H146 does not need further confirmation. The remaining near-threshold candidates (especially H198 and H200) would likely receive wider independent intervals that could not resolve whether the lower bound crosses the 10 percent threshold, and several other candidates are conditional interactions with wide intervals. Instead, I registered focused follow-ups: feature_048 on outcome_009 in the feature_021=1 stratum and its interaction with feature_021, to test whether the outcome_009 feature_048 signal is concentrated in the feature_021=0 complement, and a targeted interaction of feature_065 on outcome_015 by feature_019 level_002 within feature_021=0 to test whether H200 is localization rather than chance. All accepted or rejected conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset, not causal claims or claims about external generalizability.",
 "research_notes": "Iteration 14 appraise update. No new discoveries accepted. The only decisive validated finding remains H146: feature_021=1 versus 0 has a very large positive signed contrast on outcome_006 (weaker dependency, since scores are negative). Accepted stratum claims (H168, H169, H173, H174, H180, H182) are restatements of that dominant axis, not independent predictors.\n\nNew due assessments closed as follows: H192 unresolved/closed (outcome_009 feature_048 interaction by feature_019 level_002: point near threshold, CI crosses null); H193 reject/closed (outcome_015 feature_065 interaction by feature_021=1: opposite/null); H194 reject/closed (outcome_015 feature_065 in feature_021=1: null/opposite); H195 unresolved/closed (outcome_014 feature_065 interaction by feature_021=1: positive but very imprecise); H196 reject/closed (outcome_005 feature_021 interaction by feature_019 level_002: below threshold and crosses null); H197 reject/closed (outcome_006 feature_021 interaction by feature_065: near zero, no support for modification despite wide interval); H198 unresolved/closed (outcome_009 feature_048 in feature_021=0: positive lower bound but CI does not clear threshold); H199 reject/closed (outcome_015 feature_048 in feature_021=0: below threshold, crosses null); H200 unresolved/closed (outcome_015 feature_065 in feature_019 level_002 and feature_021=0: above threshold point, lower bound below threshold); H201 reject/closed (outcome_014 feature_065 double subgroup: tiny null); H202 unresolved/closed (outcome_014 feature_065 high log-PFS stratum: positive but extremely wide); H204 reject/closed (outcome_006 feature_055 level_004 within feature_021=0: near-zero screen).\n\nActive old near-threshold claims H186, H190, and H191 were closed as unresolved; they remain plausible but not clinically established and the new targeted tests were more decisive than additional broad follow-up.\n\nThresholds used: outcome_009 ~0.0256, outcome_015 ~0.0250, outcome_005 ~0.0250, outcome_006 ~0.0175, outcome_014 ~0.0093. Acceptance requires signed CI excluding null and lower bound above approximate threshold; point estimate alone is insufficient when the CI includes sub-threshold effects.\n\nNew proposals registered: (1) feature_048 level_003 vs level_001 on outcome_009 in feature_021=1; (2) interaction of that feature_048 contrast by feature_021=1, direction positive if the signal is concentrated in feature_021=0 complement; (3) feature_065 on outcome_015 interaction by feature_019 level_002 within feature_021=0. These aim to localize H198/H200 rather than broaden multiplicity. No validation requested because validation intervals are wider and would likely not resolve borderline threshold questions. All claims remain dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable effects."
